## Making a chart
Here is something about this

### Standard charts
Most places use **[Datawrapper](https://www.datawrapper.de/)** to make the standard bar, line, etc. 
Pros: It works and looks great, has accessibility built in and is responsove (works on mobile) out of the box.
Cons: Datawrapper is hosting your graphics so if they go under, no one knows what will happen to most of the graphics in major newsrooms. 

Many places have am enterprise Datawrapper account which lets you add customize the charts such as fonts and spacing. If you start poking around on major newspapers websites, you will Datawrapper everywhere in journalism (such as at the NYT, Reuters, and Washington Post to name a few).

The free tier of Datawrapper is generous for hobbyiest or student newsrooms.

[datawrapper example of simple and more complex]


**[Flourish](https://flourish.studio/)** is another popular option. Less design forward (I don't think they look as nice out of the box) but have much more creative chart types and also other features such as scrollytelling.

Other tools like Google Sheets, Excel, Python (Altair, Vega), R (ggplot) are great for exploration and understanding data but are much more challenging to use for publishable graphics. I would recommend against this. 

### Custom graphics with code

Almost all graphics made with code use some flavor of Javascipt. Coding with AI may change this very soon, but learning Javascipt is mandatory for making interactive code based graphics on the web.

**[D3](https://d3js.org/)**, a Javascipt package, has become the foundational tool for making graphics. While technically its simply a tool to manipulate the DOM and SVGs, it have been heavily adopted by the data viz community to create data driven graphics.

There have been countless tools build on top of D3, and in all likelihood, your favorite data viz tool probably uses D3 under the hood (including things like Datawrapper).

While not necessary for building graphics, much of web development today is done in frameworks, which you can think of as large scale packages for Javascipt. All frameworks are slightly differnet but primarily help manage state and faciliate creating resuauble components. **[React](https://react.dev/)** is the most popular overall, but I prefer **[Svelte](https://svelte.dev/)** which has become popular in the data viz community. **[Observable Framework](https://observablehq.github.io/framework/)** is another option that uses vanilla (meaning no framework) Javascipt. This is similar to a Jupyter Notebook for those familiar with Python or R Markdown. 

[example of d3 graphic]


### Custom graphics not with code

Many designers use **[Adobe Illustrator](https://www.adobe.com/products/illustrator.html)** to create pixel perfect graphics that you cant make with a standard charting tool like Datawrapper or Flourish. **[Figma](https://www.figma.com/)** is the new kid on the block and I much prefer it. Its faster,easier to use in my opinion, collaborative, free for the most part and pumping out new features constantly.  

Once you have your design you need to get it on the web, would ideally like the test to actually be html on the page for accessability and SEO and want your graphics to be responsive (adapt/ change for desktop and mobile size screens). This is where ai2html and figma2html come in. **[ai2html](http://ai2html.org/)** (as in Adobe Illustrator, not artificial intelligence) is an open-source script developed at the New York Times to converts Illustrator documents into html and css. There are lots good guides but I would recommend [Jonathaon Soma's guide](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLewNEVDy7gq3MSrrO3eMEW8PhGMEVh2X2) who teaches data journalism at Columbia's Journalism School.

Generally the process looks like

1. Use a charting tool to create an SVG as a starting place, such as making a chart in D3 or Datawrapper and exporting  (or start from scratch)
2. Edit the graphic in a vector based software such as Illustrator or Figma
3. Make different artboard sizes for desktop and mobile
4. Export the created graphic with ai2html or figma2html to convert it to an html page that handles breakpoints and resizing.
5. Embed the output into an iframe or directly on a web page.

### Designing graphics

Fonts, colors, spacing, the role of titles and subtitles

Creating a style guide, different philopshies, such as the Economist that used very strict style, rigid set of colors all the way to the New York Times which gives their graphics teams a huge amount of freedom design across a wide range of colors.
This [guide from Datawrapper](https://www.datawrapper.de/blog/colors-for-data-vis-style-guides) does a great job laying out the differnet philopshies of style guides


## Maps

Once again **[Datawrapper](https://www.datawrapper.de/)** is an inceidble tool for most of your mapping needs

As for mapping, **[Maplibre](https://maplibre.org/)**, an open source fork of Mapbox, is the clear winner for interactive maps. ALong with PMTiles, they can handle almsot an unlimited amount of data with strong performaance. (ben welsh pm tiles). There is also **[d3-geo](https://observablehq.com/@d3/-/collection/d3-geo)**

[example of maplibre]

[Natural Earth](https://www.naturalearthdata.com/downloads/) has reputable, free boundaries for most of your mapping needs (countries, oceans, etc[[]])


## Working with data

Most of the time, a tool like Excel or Google Sheets is enough to be able to load a CSV, explore the data, join with other data, or make a few transforamtions with pivot tables or forulams. Google Sheets also has the benefit of being collaborative by default.

AP Datakit

However, sometimes you need a more power tool to process data repeatedly, open a very large file, or do more advanced transforamtions. This is where a scripting language comes in. The key here is reproducabiltiy. You should be able to give me your process and I should be able to recreate your finding or output data. My prefered tool is Python but R works just as well in most cases

### My favorite Python tools
* UV as a package manager
* Polars for fast 

